        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.287,
            0.878,
            0.327
          ],
          "content": "？ **Is it possible for all the eight angles to have different measurements? Why, why not?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.34,
            0.881,
            0.38
          ],
          "content": "**Ans:** No, because $\\angle 1 = \\angle 3 , \\angle 2 = \\angle 4 , \\angle 5 = \\angle 7 ,$ and $\\angle 6 = \\angle 8$ (vertically opposite angles are equal)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.162,
            0.393,
            0.683,
            0.414
          ],
          "content": "**What about five different angles — 6, 5, 4, 3 and 2?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.428,
            0.385,
            0.447
          ],
          "content": "**Ans:** No, because $\\angle 2 = \\angle 4.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.464,
            0.253,
            0.484
          ],
          "content": "**Page No. 119**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.497,
            0.303,
            0.518
          ],
          "content": "？ **Figure it Out**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.161,
            0.541,
            0.874,
            0.605
          ],
          "content": "**Can you draw a line parallel to l, that goes through point A? How will you do it with the tools from your geometry box? Describe your method.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.378,
            0.621,
            0.61,
            0.702
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_29_image_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.721,
            0.169,
            0.738
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Ans:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.161,
            0.742,
            0.882,
            0.785
          ],
          "content": "Tools required: Ruler, Set-squares (right-angled triangle), Pencil, eraser **Steps:**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.196,
            0.801,
            0.753,
            0.82
          ],
          "content": "1. Place the set square so that one side is along the line l."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.195,
            0.821,
            0.852,
            0.858
          ],
          "content": "2. Hold the ruler against the other side of the set square so that the ruler won’t move."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.196,
            0.859,
            0.863,
            0.879
          ],
          "content": "3. Slide the set square along the ruler until one side reaches point A."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.196,
            0.879,
            0.816,
            0.899
          ],
          "content": "4. Draw a line along the edge of the set square through point A."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
